(window.CE_PACK=window.CE_PACK||{})["Final Year Project I"]=Object.assign((window.CE_PACK||{})["Final Year Project I"]||{},{docs:{"assign":"# Final Year Project I: Assignments with Worked Solutions\n\nThese assignments mirror the deliverables of FYP I. Written answers should be specific to an engineering project; the model solutions show the expected level of detail, not the only acceptable wording. All numerical answers were computed and checked in Python (critical path, PERT, earned value, NPV, sample size, uncertainty propagation, t-tests).\n\n# Assignment 1: Problem Definition, Proposal and Literature Review (70 marks)\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1. Problem statement and objectives (20 marks)** A student writes: \"My project is to make a better solar charger.\" (a) List four weaknesses of this statement [4]. (b) Rewrite it as a problem statement with context, gap and measurable target for a project of your choice [6]. (c) Write one aim and three SMART objectives, each mapped to a deliverable [6]. (d) Give three items that are explicitly out of scope and two assumptions [4].\n\n**Q2. Search strategy and screening (18 marks)** (a) Write a Boolean search string for \"low-cost air-quality monitoring using IoT sensors\" with synonyms and explain each operator [6]. (b) A search returns 2,400 records; 600 survive the title screen, 150 the abstract screen and 40 are read in full. Compute the yield at each stage and overall, and estimate the reading time at 2.5 hours per paper [5]. (c) List four inclusion/exclusion criteria [4]. (d) What is snowballing? [3]\n\n**Q3. Synthesis and critical review (14 marks)** (a) Give the columns of a synthesis matrix you would use for ten papers on battery state-of-charge estimation [4]. (b) Rewrite this sentence from a weak review into a critical comparison: \"Smith used a Kalman filter. Jones used a neural network. Lee used coulomb counting.\" (use hypothetical results) [6]. (c) State what a \"research gap\" is and give an example [4].\n\n**Q4. Citation and integrity (10 marks)** (a) Format an IEEE reference for: authors A. Rahman and L. Chen, title \"A low-power humidity sensor node for greenhouses\", journal IEEE Sensors Journal, volume 22, issue 7, pages 6401 to 6410, year 2022 [3]. (b) Distinguish quoting, paraphrasing and plagiarism with an example of each [4]. (c) State two rules for using AI writing tools responsibly in a project report [3].\n\n**Q5. Proposal review (8 marks)** List eight sections of a good proposal and say what question each answers.\n\n## Solutions\n\n**Q1.**\n(a) Weaknesses: no problem or user is identified; \"better\" is undefined and unmeasurable; no baseline or comparison; no scope (power rating, application, cost); no gap relative to existing chargers; no deliverable or time frame.\n(b) Example: \"Off-grid farm workers in region X charge phones and lamps from diesel generators costing about $0.40 per kWh equivalent; commercial solar chargers above 20 W cost over $120 and lose 25 to 40 percent of the panel energy in conversion. This project will design a 20 W solar charge controller with MPPT that achieves at least 92 percent conversion efficiency at a target cost under $35.\" It has context, gap and numbers.\n(c) Aim: to design, build and evaluate a low-cost MPPT solar charge controller for 12 V lead-acid batteries. Objectives: **O1** (week 6) by simulation, select an MPPT algorithm and converter topology that reach at least 92 percent efficiency over 20 to 100 percent load (deliverable: simulation report). **O2** (week 12) design and build a prototype with bill of materials under $35 (deliverable: schematic, PCB, BOM). **O3** (week 18) test the prototype over a range of irradiance and report efficiency with uncertainty compared with a simple PWM charger (deliverable: test report).\n(d) Out of scope: lithium chemistries, grid-tie inverters, a smartphone app, commercial certification. Assumptions: panel open-circuit voltage below 22 V; ambient temperature between 0 and 45 °C.\n\n**Q2.**\n(a) `(\"air quality\" OR \"air pollution\" OR \"particulate matter\" OR PM2.5) AND (monitor* OR sensing OR \"sensor network\") AND (\"low-cost\" OR \"low cost\" OR \"inexpensive\") AND (IoT OR \"Internet of Things\" OR \"wireless sensor\")`. OR joins synonyms (widens), AND narrows by requiring all concepts, quotation marks keep phrases together, the asterisk truncates (monitor, monitoring, monitors), parentheses group terms.\n(b) Yields: title screen 600/2400 = **25 percent**; abstract screen 150/600 = **25 percent**; full reading 40/150 = **26.7 percent**; overall 40/2400 = **1.67 percent**. Reading time = 40 × 2.5 = **100 hours** (plan this in the schedule).\n(c) Examples: published 2015 or later; peer-reviewed journal or conference; reports measured sensor accuracy or cost; written in English; excludes papers with no experimental validation; excludes duplicates.\n(d) Snowballing finds further papers from a good paper: **backward** (read its reference list) and **forward** (find later papers that cite it, using Google Scholar or Scopus).\n\n**Q3.**\n(a) Columns: citation; year; battery chemistry; estimation method; data/test conditions; reported accuracy (e.g. RMSE in percent SoC); computational cost; hardware needed; limitations; relevance to my project.\n(b) \"Smith [3] used an extended Kalman filter and achieved a 2.1 percent SoC error at 25 °C but did not test below 10 °C, where cell parameters change sharply. Jones [4] trained a neural network that reduced error to 1.4 percent, at the cost of 5,000 labelled cycles and 1.2 MB of model memory, which exceeds our microcontroller budget. Lee [5] applied coulomb counting, which is cheap but drifts by 5 percent per day without recalibration. None combined low memory with low-temperature validation, which motivates the approach proposed here.\"\n(c) A research gap is a question or condition the existing work has not adequately addressed (for example no study validates low-cost SoC estimation on a 8-bit-class microcontroller below 0 °C). It justifies why the project is needed.\n\n**Q4.**\n(a) `[1] A. Rahman and L. Chen, \"A low-power humidity sensor node for greenhouses,\" IEEE Sensors J., vol. 22, no. 7, pp. 6401-6410, 2022.`\n(b) **Quoting**: reproducing exact words in quotation marks with a citation (\"The device 'consumes 4 µA in sleep mode' [1].\"). **Paraphrasing**: restating the idea in your own words and citing it (\"The node's sleep current is only a few microamps [1].\"). **Plagiarism**: using the author's wording, figure or idea without credit (copying the sentence without quotation marks or citation).\n(c) Any two: disclose use as the institution's policy requires; verify every fact, number and reference (AI tools can invent citations); never submit text or code you cannot explain or defend; keep your own analysis and results as your work.\n\n**Q5.** Title and abstract (what is the project in brief?); introduction and background (why does it matter?); problem statement (what exactly is the gap?); aim and objectives (what will be achieved?); literature review (what is known and what is missing?); methodology and design approach (how will it be done?); work plan with milestones (when?); resources, budget and risks (what is needed and what could go wrong?). Also expected outcomes and evaluation, ethics and safety, and references.\n\n# Assignment 2: Requirements, Concept Selection and Design Reliability (70 marks)\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1. Writing requirements (16 marks)** Rewrite each vague requirement as a measurable, verifiable requirement and state how it is verified: (a) \"The sensor node should last a long time on battery.\" (b) \"The system must be fast.\" (c) \"The enclosure must be rugged.\" (d) \"The interface should be user friendly.\" [16]\n\n**Q2. Prioritization and traceability (12 marks)** (a) Classify with MoSCoW: wireless reporting every 15 minutes; a mobile app; operation from -10 to 50 °C; OTA firmware updates; a colour touch-screen [5]. (b) Construct a traceability table for three requirements R1 (battery life at least 2 years), R2 (humidity accuracy ±3 percent RH), R3 (report delivered within 60 s) listing design elements and tests [7].\n\n**Q3. Concept selection (20 marks)** Choose a radio for a farm sensor. Criteria and weights: range 0.35, power 0.30, cost 0.20, ease of development 0.15. Scores (range, power, cost, ease): BLE (5, 9, 9, 9), LoRa (9, 8, 6, 6), Wi-Fi (6, 3, 8, 9), Zigbee (7, 8, 7, 6). (a) Compute the weighted scores and rank [6]. (b) Repeat with weights (0.2, 0.4, 0.2, 0.2) and with equal weights [6]. (c) Comment on robustness and give a recommendation with a justification that also uses requirements, not only the matrix [5]. (d) State one weakness of weighted matrices [3].\n\n**Q4. Reliability (14 marks)** A node has four subsystems in series with MTBFs of 40,000 h, 25,000 h, 100,000 h and 60,000 h (exponential failures). (a) Compute the system failure rate and MTBF [4]. (b) Compute the reliability over 2,000 h [3]. (c) The weakest subsystem is duplicated in parallel; recompute the 2,000 h reliability [4]. (d) If mean repair time is 8 h and MTBF is 20,000 h, find availability and annual downtime [3].\n\n**Q5. Design review (8 marks)** List six items to bring to a design review and four questions a reviewer should ask.\n\n**Q6. Block diagram and interfaces (4 marks)** List the four pieces of information needed to define an interface between two subsystems.\n\n## Solutions\n\n**Q1.**\n(a) \"The node shall operate for at least 24 months from two AA lithium cells (3,000 mAh nominal) reporting every 15 minutes at 25 °C, giving an average current of at most 120 µA.\" Verified by measuring average current with a power analyser over 24 h and computing life with a 20 percent derating.\n(b) \"The controller shall respond to a 10 percent step in setpoint with a settling time (2 percent) below 5 s.\" Verified by step-response tests recorded at 1 kHz, five runs.\n(c) \"The enclosure shall meet IP65 and survive a 1 m drop onto concrete on six faces without loss of function.\" Verified by IP test or an equivalent spray test and a drop test.\n(d) \"A first-time user shall complete the pairing procedure in under 3 minutes without help, with at least 8 of 10 test users succeeding.\" Verified by a usability test with ten users.\n\n**Q2.**\n(a) **Must**: wireless reporting every 15 minutes; operation from -10 to 50 °C (stated environment). **Should**: OTA firmware updates. **Could**: a mobile app. **Won't (this time)**: colour touch-screen (high cost and power, little value).\n(b) R1 → design: low-power MCU with deep sleep, duty-cycled radio, power budget; test: 24 h average-current measurement. R2 → design: calibrated capacitive sensor, 12-bit ADC, temperature compensation; test: comparison with a reference hygrometer at 20, 50, 80 percent RH, with uncertainty. R3 → design: radio and protocol (retries, acknowledgement), server timestamp; test: send 1,000 messages and check that 99 percent arrive within 60 s. Each row also records owner and status.\n\n**Q3.**\n(a) BLE = 0.35·5 + 0.30·9 + 0.20·9 + 0.15·9 = 1.75 + 2.70 + 1.80 + 1.35 = **7.60**; LoRa = 3.15 + 2.40 + 1.20 + 0.90 = **7.65**; Wi-Fi = 2.10 + 0.90 + 1.60 + 1.35 = **5.95**; Zigbee = 2.45 + 2.40 + 1.40 + 0.90 = **7.15**. Ranking: LoRa (7.65), BLE (7.60), Zigbee (7.15), Wi-Fi (5.95).\n(b) Weights (0.2, 0.4, 0.2, 0.2): BLE **8.2**, LoRa **7.4**, Wi-Fi **5.8**, Zigbee **7.2**. Equal weights: BLE **8.0**, LoRa **7.25**, Wi-Fi **6.5**, Zigbee **7.0**.\n(c) LoRa and BLE are close in the baseline (7.65 versus 7.60) and the order flips when power is weighted more heavily or the weights are equal, so the matrix alone does not decide. The deciding factor is the requirement: if the nodes are spread over several hundred metres to kilometres of farmland, BLE's range (score 5) fails a **must** requirement and LoRa should be chosen despite the cost; if all nodes are within tens of metres of a gateway, BLE wins. State the range requirement explicitly, treat it as a pass/fail gate before scoring, and report the sensitivity.\n(d) Scores and weights are subjective and can be tuned to a preferred answer; criteria may be correlated (double counting); scores hide thresholds (a fatal weakness can be offset by strengths elsewhere); results are ordinal approximations treated as numbers.\n\n**Q4.**\n(a) λ = 1/40,000 + 1/25,000 + 1/100,000 + 1/60,000 = 2.5×10⁻⁵ + 4.0×10⁻⁵ + 1.0×10⁻⁵ + 1.667×10⁻⁵ = 9.167×10⁻⁵ per hour; **MTBF = 10,909 h** (about 1.25 years).\n(b) R(2000) = e^(-9.167×10⁻⁵ × 2000) = e^(-0.1833) = **0.832** (the product of the individual reliabilities 0.951 × 0.923 × 0.980 × 0.967).\n(c) Step 1: The weak subsystem alone has R = e^(-2000/25000) = 0.923.\nStep 2: Two in parallel: 1 - (1 - 0.923)² = 0.994.\nStep 3: System reliability = 0.994 × (0.951 × 0.980 × 0.967) = **0.896** (up from 0.832).\nStep 4: Redundancy costs mass, power, money and complexity (common-cause failures reduce the benefit).\n(d) Availability = MTBF/(MTBF + MTTR) = 20,000/20,008 = **0.9996**; downtime ≈ 8760 × 0.0004 = **3.5 h per year**.\n\n**Q5.** Bring: requirements and traceability table; architecture and block diagram; interface definitions; simulation or analysis results (power, timing, thermal); the decision matrix and alternatives considered; the risk register, BOM and cost; the test plan. Reviewer's questions: Which requirement does this satisfy and how will it be verified? What happens when this fails? What assumptions are untested? What is the riskiest part and how is it reduced? How were the alternatives compared? Is it safe, standards-compliant and maintainable?\n\n**Q6.** The physical/electrical definition (connector, voltage levels, timing); the protocol and message format (framing, data types, units); the behavior under error and timeouts (retries, defaults); and the ownership and version of the interface specification (who may change it).\n\n# Assignment 3: Planning, Cost, Statistics and Professional Practice (70 marks)\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1. Critical path (18 marks)** Tasks (weeks): A scope 2 (none); B survey 3 (A); C requirements 2 (A); D design 4 (B, C); E simulation 3 (D); F order and receive parts 4 (D); G build 3 (F); H integration 2 (E, G); I testing 3 (H); J report 2 (I). (a) Compute earliest and latest start/finish and float for every task [10]. (b) Identify the critical path and total duration [3]. (c) A supplier delay adds 2 weeks to F; what happens? What if E takes 3 weeks longer? [5]\n\n**Q2. PERT (10 marks)** Three sequential tasks have (optimistic, most likely, pessimistic) durations in weeks of (3, 5, 10), (1, 2, 3) and (4, 6, 14). Find the expected duration and standard deviation of each task and of the chain, and the probabilities of finishing within 12, 14 and 16 weeks [10].\n\n**Q3. Earned value (10 marks)** A $30,000 project is 50 percent through its planned schedule, 45 percent of the work is done and $16,000 has been spent. Compute PV, EV, CPI, SPI, CV, SV, EAC (using BAC/CPI), ETC and VAC, and interpret [10].\n\n**Q4. Economics and risk (12 marks)** (a) A product development costs $8,000 and returns $3,000 per year for four years; discount rate 8 percent. Compute the NPV, simple payback and IRR [6]. (b) What annual benefit makes the NPV zero? What is the NPV if the benefit is only $2,000? [3] (c) Rank these risks by likelihood × impact: late parts (4, 4), scope creep (3, 4), illness (2, 3), simulation mismatch (3, 3), lost data (1, 5), and compute the expected cost of a risk with a 40 percent chance of costing $2,000 and a 20 percent chance of costing $5,000 [3].\n\n**Q5. Measurement and statistics (14 marks)** (a) R = V/I with V = 5.00 ± 0.05 V and I = 0.250 ± 0.005 A: compute R, its uncertainty (quadrature) and the worst-case uncertainty [4]. (b) Eight readings of a nominal 5 V rail: 4.92, 5.05, 4.98, 5.10, 5.01, 4.95, 5.07, 4.99. Compute the mean, standard deviation, standard error and a 95 percent CI (t₀.₉₇₅,₇ = 2.365) [5]. (c) Two filter designs give accuracies (percent) A = 85, 88, 90, 84, 87, 86 and B = 91, 93, 89, 94, 92, 90. Compute the means and standard deviations and state the conclusion of a t-test (t = -4.14, p = 0.002) [3]. (d) Sample size to estimate a mean with σ = 0.8 and E = 0.2 at 95 percent, and a proportion with E = 0.04 [2].\n\n**Q6. Professional practice (6 marks)** A teammate has not contributed for three weeks and a deliverable is due in ten days. Describe the steps you would take, and name two ethical issues if you copy a result from a previous student's report.\n\n## Solutions\n\n**Q1.**\n(a) Forward pass: A 0-2; B 2-5; C 2-4; D 5-9 (waits for B); E 9-12; F 9-13; G 13-16; H 16-18 (waits for G); I 18-21; J 21-23. Backward pass (project length 23): J 21-23; I 18-21; H 16-18; G 13-16; F 9-13; E latest 13-16; D 5-9; C latest 3-5; B 2-5; A 0-2. Floats: A 0; B 0; **C 1**; D 0; **E 4**; F 0; G 0; H 0; I 0; J 0.\n(b) Critical path: **A-B-D-F-G-H-I-J = 2 + 3 + 4 + 4 + 3 + 2 + 3 + 2 = 23 weeks**.\n(c) F is critical, so a 2-week supplier delay makes the project **25 weeks**. E has 4 weeks of float, so a 3-week overrun (6 weeks) does not delay H (E would finish at week 15, before G at 16): project stays at 23 weeks, and the float falls to 1 week. Mitigation for F: order early, qualify an alternate supplier, or pre-build using development boards.\n\n**Q2.** Step 1: Expected duration (o + 4m + p)/6 and σ = (p - o)/6: task 1: (3 + 20 + 10)/6 = **5.5**, σ = **1.17**; task 2: (1 + 8 + 3)/6 = **2.0**, σ = **0.33**; task 3: (4 + 24 + 14)/6 = **7.0**, σ = **1.67**.\nStep 2: Chain: mean = **14.5 weeks**; variance = 1.36 + 0.11 + 2.78 = 4.25, σ = **2.06 weeks**.\nStep 3: P(T ≤ 12) = Φ((12 - 14.5)/2.06) = Φ(-1.21) = **0.113**; P(T ≤ 14) = Φ(-0.24) = **0.404**; P(T ≤ 16) = Φ(0.73) = **0.767**.\nStep 4: The 90 percent date is 14.5 + 1.28 × 2.06 = **17.1 weeks**.\nStep 5: Plan to the 80 to 90 percent date, not the mean.\n\n**Q3.** Step 1: PV = 0.50 × 30,000 = **$15,000**; EV = 0.45 × 30,000 = **$13,500**; AC = $16,000.\nStep 2: CPI = EV/AC = **0.844**; SPI = EV/PV = **0.90**; CV = EV - AC = **-$2,500**; SV = EV - PV = **-$1,500**.\nStep 3: EAC = BAC/CPI = **$35,556**; ETC = EAC - AC = **$19,556**; VAC = BAC - EAC = **-$5,556**.\nStep 4: Interpretation: the project is behind schedule (10 percent) and over budget (each dollar buys 84 cents of work); if the trend continues it will overrun by about $5,600.\nStep 5: An alternative forecast assuming the remaining work goes to plan is EAC = AC + (BAC - EV) = $32,500.\nStep 6: Report both and take corrective action (reduce scope, add resources carefully, renegotiate).\n\n**Q4.**\n(a) Step 1: NPV = -8,000 + 3,000 × (1/1.08 + 1/1.08² + 1/1.08³ + 1/1.08⁴) = -8,000 + 3,000 × 3.3121 = **+$1,936**.\nStep 2: Simple payback = 8,000/3,000 = **2.67 years** (the discounted cumulative cash flow is still -$269 at the end of year 3, so the discounted payback is in year 4).\nStep 3: IRR = **18.5 percent**, above the 8 percent hurdle rate.\n(b) Break-even annual benefit = 8,000/3.3121 = **$2,415**. With $2,000 per year: NPV = -8,000 + 2,000 × 3.3121 = **-$1,376** (reject): the project is sensitive to the benefit estimate, so test it.\n(c) Scores: late parts **16**, scope creep **12**, simulation mismatch **9**, illness **6**, lost data **5**. Expected cost = 0.4 × 2,000 + 0.2 × 5,000 = **$1,800** contingency.\n\n**Q5.**\n(a) R = 5.00/0.250 = **20.0 Ω**; relative uncertainties 1 percent and 2 percent; combined √(1² + 2²) = 2.24 percent → **±0.45 Ω**; worst case (1 + 2) percent → ±0.60 Ω.\n(b) Mean = 5.009 V; s = 0.0613 V; SE = 0.0613/√8 = 0.0217 V; CI = 5.009 ± 2.365 × 0.0217 = **(4.958, 5.060) V**. The nominal 5.000 V lies inside the interval, so the rail is consistent with 5 V at the 95 percent level.\n(c) A: mean 86.7, s = 2.16; B: mean 91.5, s = 1.87. Difference 4.8 percentage points; t = -4.14 (about 10 degrees of freedom), p = 0.002 < 0.05: B is **significantly more accurate**; report also the effect size and that six runs per design is a small sample.\n(d) n = (1.96 × 0.8/0.2)² = 61.5 → **62** samples; proportion: n = 1.96² × 0.25/0.04² = 600.25 → **601**.\n\n**Q6.** Steps: talk to the teammate privately and find out the cause (illness, overload, misunderstanding); agree specific tasks with dates and a way to check progress; document contributions in the logbook; inform the supervisor early if there is no improvement and ask for guidance on redistributing work; reduce scope if needed using MoSCoW; do not let the deadline pass without telling anyone. Copying a previous student's result is **plagiarism and possibly fabrication** if you did not obtain the data yourself: it misattributes credit, misleads readers about what you did, and breaks academic integrity policy; if you want to use earlier work, cite it as a source and do your own verification.\n","exam":"# Final Year Project I: Final Examination\n\nDuration: 3 hours. Calculator and a one-page formula sheet allowed. Total: 100 marks.\n- Section A: 20 multiple-choice questions, 1 mark each (20 marks). Answer all.\n- Section B: 8 short questions, 5 marks each (40 marks). Answer all.\n- Section C: 5 long questions, 10 marks each. Answer any four (40 marks).\n\nUseful: z₀.₉₇₅ = 1.96; z₀.₉₀ = 1.2816; Φ(0.55) = 0.709, Φ(1.19) = 0.883, Φ(-0.67) = 0.251; e^-0.1 = 0.905.\n\n## Section A: Multiple Choice (20 marks)\n\n**1.** The aim of a project differs from its objectives in that the aim is\nA) a single task  B) the budget  C) the reference list  D) the overall purpose, achieved through a set of specific objectives\n\n**2.** Which is the best objective?\nA) Study sensors  B) By week 12, build a prototype that measures temperature within ±0.5 °C over 0 to 50 °C  C) Improve the design  D) Make a good product\n\n**3.** Snowballing in a literature search means\nA) copying a review  B) following reference lists and citations from known good papers  C) deleting old papers  D) searching only on Google\n\n**4.** A synthesis matrix is used to\nA) compare sources on common criteria and reveal gaps  B) check spelling  C) draw circuits  D) schedule tasks\n\n**5.** A traceability matrix links\nA) papers to authors  B) team members to hours  C) requirements to design elements and tests  D) tasks to money only\n\n**6.** Validation checks that\nA) the code compiles  B) the document is formatted  C) the product meets the real needs of the user  D) the schedule is met\n\n**7.** Float on a non-critical task means\nA) it is optional  B) it is more expensive  C) it must start first  D) it can be delayed by that amount without delaying the project\n\n**8.** PERT standard deviation of one task is approximated by\nA) (p - o)/6  B) m/6  C) (o + 4m + p)/6  D) (o + p)/2\n\n**9.** Adding a redundant parallel unit to a component generally\nA) removes all failures  B) raises reliability but adds cost, mass and complexity  C) lowers system reliability  D) has no effect\n\n**10.** In earned value, CPI less than 1 indicates\nA) the work is costing more than planned  B) the project is ahead of schedule  C) the project is under budget  D) there is no risk\n\n**11.** A project with NPV less than zero at the required discount rate\nA) has an infinite payback  B) has IRR above the hurdle rate  C) should generally not be accepted on financial grounds alone  D) is risk free\n\n**12.** A risk register should contain\nA) the team's salaries  B) only completed risks  C) just the project title  D) risks with likelihood, impact, owner and mitigation\n\n**13.** The best way to avoid scope creep is to\nA) agree and freeze the scope and control changes formally  B) accept every request  C) never talk to the client  D) delete the plan\n\n**14.** Reporting a result with an uncertainty is important because it\nA) hides errors  B) replaces the method section  C) lengthens the report  D) shows how reliable the number is\n\n**15.** For comparing the means of two independent samples with unequal variances use\nA) the median  B) a Welch t-test  C) a chi-square goodness-of-fit test  D) a linear regression\n\n**16.** Data leakage in a machine-learning project means\nA) the battery is empty  B) test information influences training or tuning  C) the data are too small  D) the server is hacked\n\n**17.** Which action is academically dishonest?\nA) discussing ideas with your supervisor  B) presenting another person's results as your own  C) citing sources  D) keeping a logbook\n\n**18.** Ethical approval is normally required when a project\nA) collects data from human participants  B) uses a microcontroller  C) has a Gantt chart  D) uses open-source libraries\n\n**19.** A good abstract\nA) lists every reference  B) summarizes problem, method, main results and conclusion in about 150 to 250 words  C) contains only background  D) is written before the project starts and never changed\n\n**20.** The best time to start writing the report is\nA) after the examiner's feedback  B) the final week  C) after the viva  D) from the start, drafting sections as work is done\n\n## Section B: Short Questions (40 marks)\n\n**B1. Scope (5 marks)** For a project \"A low-cost air-quality node\", list three items in scope, three out of scope and two assumptions, and explain why defining scope matters.\n\n**B2. Requirements (5 marks)** Rewrite \"the system must be reliable\" and \"the display must be readable\" as verifiable requirements with a test method for each.\n\n**B3. Screening (5 marks)** A search returns 2,000 records; 500 pass the title screen, 100 the abstract screen and 25 are read in full. Compute each stage's yield and the overall yield, and the hours needed at 2.5 hours per full paper.\n\n**B4. Decision matrix (5 marks)** Criteria weights: cost 0.4, performance 0.3, risk 0.2, time 0.1. Scores (cost, performance, risk, time): P = (6, 8, 7, 5), Q = (9, 5, 6, 7), R = (7, 7, 8, 8). Compute the weighted scores and choose; and the winner with equal weights.\n\n**B5. Float (5 marks)** Explain the difference between the critical path and a near-critical path and why managers monitor both. What does negative float mean?\n\n**B6. PERT (5 marks)** A task has optimistic 2, most likely 4 and pessimistic 8 weeks. Compute its expected duration and standard deviation, and explain why the expected value is greater than the most likely value.\n\n**B7. Hypothesis test (5 marks)** Two filters are compared with 10 runs each: A has mean accuracy 52.0 and s = 4.0; B has mean 55.0 and s = 4.0. Compute t = difference/√(sA²/n + sB²/n), compare with 2.10 (critical value, 18 degrees of freedom, 5 percent two-sided) and conclude. What would you do next?\n\n**B8. Ethics (5 marks)** A student uses survey data from classmates without telling them the purpose and keeps names in the spreadsheet. List the ethical problems and the corrective actions.\n\n## Section C: Long Questions (answer any four, 40 marks)\n\n**C1. Proposal critique (10 marks)** A draft proposal says: \"This project will design a robot arm that is the best on the market. It will use AI and will be finished by the end of the year. Budget: to be decided.\" (a) Identify at least six weaknesses [6]. (b) Rewrite the problem statement, the aim and two SMART objectives for a plausible version of the project (for example a low-cost 3-axis arm for pick-and-place of 200 g objects) [4].\n\n**C2. Literature review plan (10 marks)** You are reviewing \"machine-learning methods for predicting battery remaining useful life\". (a) Write a search string and list the databases [3]. (b) Define four inclusion/exclusion criteria and the screening stages [3]. (c) Describe a synthesis matrix with its columns and how it reveals a gap [4].\n\n**C3. Requirements, concept selection and reliability (10 marks)** (a) A sensor node's subsystems are in series: sensor MTBF 30,000 h, microcontroller 80,000 h, radio 20,000 h. Compute the failure rate, MTBF and 1,000 h reliability [4]. (b) The radio is duplicated in parallel; compute the new 1,000 h system reliability [3]. (c) Write three measurable requirements for the node (power, accuracy, communication) with verification methods [3].\n\n**C4. Scheduling (10 marks)** Tasks (weeks): A 3 (none); B 4 (A); C 2 (A); D 3 (B); E 5 (B, C); F 2 (D, E); G 3 (F). (a) Compute ES, EF, LS, LF and float for every task [6]. (b) Give the critical path and duration [2]. (c) The probabilities: tasks X and Y in series have (o, m, p) = (2, 4, 8) and (3, 6, 15) weeks. Find the mean and σ of the chain and P(finish ≤ 14 weeks) [2].\n\n**C5. Cost, value and statistics (10 marks)** (a) A $50,000 project has PV = $25,000, EV = $20,000, AC = $24,000. Compute CPI, SPI, EAC (BAC/CPI), ETC and VAC, and recommend an action [4]. (b) A product costs $15,000 and returns $6,000 a year for three years, discount rate 10 percent. Compute the NPV, IRR (approximately) and the break-even annual benefit, and give a decision [4]. (c) How many people must be surveyed to estimate a proportion near 0.3 within ±0.05 at 95 percent confidence? [2]\n\n## Answer key and mark scheme\n\n### Section A\n1. D 2. B 3. B 4. A 5. C 6. C 7. D 8. A 9. B 10. A 11. C 12. D 13. A 14. D 15. B 16. B 17. B 18. A 19. B 20. D\n\n### Section B\n\n**B1.** In scope (examples): sensing PM2.5, temperature and humidity; wireless reporting to a dashboard; calibration against a reference instrument. Out of scope: a consumer mobile app, mass production and regulatory certification, indoor-air purification. Assumptions: mains or USB power is available; network coverage at the test site [3]. Defining scope sets boundaries so the work fits the time and budget, avoids scope creep, and gives a basis for evaluation and for saying \"no\" to additions [2].\n\n**B2.** \"The node shall run for 30 days continuously without a reset, and report at least 99 percent of its scheduled readings; verified by a 30-day soak test with logging\" [2.5]. \"The display shall show characters of at least 5 mm height with a contrast ratio of at least 4.5:1 and be readable from 1 m in 500 lux ambient light; verified by readability tests with five users and a lux meter\" [2.5].\n\n**B3.** Title screen 500/2000 = **25 percent**; abstract screen 100/500 = **20 percent**; full-text 25/100 = **25 percent**; overall 25/2000 = **1.25 percent** [3]. Reading time = 25 × 2.5 = **62.5 hours** [2].\n\n**B4.** P = 0.4·6 + 0.3·8 + 0.2·7 + 0.1·5 = 2.4 + 2.4 + 1.4 + 0.5 = **6.7**; Q = 3.6 + 1.5 + 1.2 + 0.7 = **7.0**; R = 2.8 + 2.1 + 1.6 + 0.8 = **7.3**; choose **R** [3]. Equal weights: P = 6.5, Q = 6.75, R = **7.5**; R wins in both, so the choice is robust [2].\n\n**B5.** The critical path is the longest path with zero float; a near-critical path has small positive float and becomes critical after a small delay [2]. Managers monitor both because delays on near-critical paths can quickly create a new critical path [1]. Negative float means the schedule is already behind the target date (the task's latest finish is earlier than its forecast finish): corrective action is required [2].\n\n**B6.** Expected = (2 + 4 × 4 + 8)/6 = **4.33 weeks**; σ = (8 - 2)/6 = **1.0 week** [3]. The distribution is skewed: the pessimistic value is much further from the most likely value than the optimistic one, so the mean lies above the mode [2].\n\n**B7.** Standard error = √(16/10 + 16/10) = √3.2 = 1.789; t = 3.0/1.789 = **1.68** [3]. Since 1.68 < 2.10 the difference is **not statistically significant** at the 5 percent level (p ≈ 0.11) [1]. Next: collect more runs (power analysis), report the confidence interval and effect size, and check whether a 3-point difference matters in practice [1].\n\n**B8.** Problems: no informed consent or disclosure of purpose; personally identifiable information stored without need; possible lack of ethical approval; risk of misuse or disclosure [3]. Corrections: obtain approval, give an information sheet and consent form, collect anonymously or remove names, store data securely, delete after the retention period, and report in aggregate [2].\n\n### Section C\n\n**C1.**\n(a) Weaknesses: \"best on the market\" is unmeasurable and unverifiable; no problem, user or gap identified; \"uses AI\" is a solution in search of a problem; no deliverables or scope; no performance targets; unrealistic or undefined time frame (\"end of the year\" with no milestones); budget absent; no literature or feasibility evidence; no risks, ethics or evaluation method [6].\n(b) Problem: \"Small workshops hand-sort 200 g parts at about 600 parts per hour with 3 percent errors; commercial pick-and-place arms cost over $5,000. This project will design a 3-axis arm costing less than $400 that handles 200 g parts.\" Aim: to design, build and evaluate a low-cost 3-axis pick-and-place arm. Objectives: **O1** by week 8, complete kinematic and torque analysis and select motors, with a simulation showing positioning error below 2 mm; **O2** by week 20, build a prototype within a $400 budget that picks and places 200 g objects at at least 6 cycles per minute with a repeatability of ±1 mm, verified in 50 trials [4].\n\n**C2.**\n(a) `(\"remaining useful life\" OR RUL OR \"state of health\") AND (battery OR \"lithium-ion\") AND (\"machine learning\" OR \"deep learning\" OR \"neural network\" OR regression) AND (predict* OR prognos* OR estimat*)`. Databases: IEEE Xplore, ScienceDirect, Scopus, Web of Science, ACM DL, Google Scholar [3].\n(b) Criteria (any four): published in the last 8 years; peer-reviewed; lithium-ion cells; reports quantitative error (RMSE or MAE) on public datasets (for example NASA or CALCE); written in English; excludes papers without validation; excludes duplicates. Stages: records identified → duplicates removed → title screen → abstract screen → full-text review → included studies, recording counts at each stage (a flow diagram) [3].\n(c) Columns: citation; year; dataset; features; algorithm; accuracy (RMSE); training data needed; computation/memory; real-time feasibility; limitations. Reading down the columns shows, for example, that most methods are validated only on one dataset at a single temperature and none runs on a microcontroller: that missing combination is the gap [4].\n\n**C3.**\n(a) λ = 1/30,000 + 1/80,000 + 1/20,000 = 3.33×10⁻⁵ + 1.25×10⁻⁵ + 5.0×10⁻⁵ = 9.58×10⁻⁵ per hour; **MTBF = 10,435 h**; R(1000) = e^(-0.0958) = **0.909** [4].\n(b) Radio alone R = e^(-1000/20000) = 0.951; two in parallel: 1 - (0.049)² = 0.9976. System = 0.9976 × e^(-1000/30000) × e^(-1000/80000) = 0.9976 × 0.9672 × 0.9876 = **0.953** (up from 0.909) [3].\n(c) Examples: \"Average current shall not exceed 150 µA at 3.3 V when reporting every 10 minutes (verified with a power analyser over 24 h)\"; \"Temperature accuracy shall be ±0.5 °C over 0 to 50 °C (verified against a reference thermometer at 5 points, n = 5 each, with uncertainty)\"; \"At least 99 percent of reports shall reach the server within 60 s at a 500 m range (verified over 1,000 transmissions)\" [3].\n\n**C4.**\n(a) A: ES 0, EF 3, LS 0, LF 3, float 0. B: 3-7, LS 3, LF 7, float 0. C: ES 3, EF 5, LS 5, LF 7, **float 2**. D: ES 7, EF 10, LS 9, LF 12, **float 2**. E: ES 7, EF 12, LS 7, LF 12, float 0. F: ES 12, EF 14, float 0. G: ES 14, EF 17, float 0 [6].\n(b) Critical path **A-B-E-F-G = 3 + 4 + 5 + 2 + 3 = 17 weeks** [2].\n(c) X: (2 + 16 + 8)/6 = 4.33, σ = 1.0; Y: (3 + 24 + 15)/6 = 7.0, σ = 2.0. Chain: mean = **11.33 weeks**, σ = √(1 + 4) = **2.24**. P(T ≤ 14) = Φ((14 - 11.33)/2.24) = Φ(1.19) = **0.883** [2].\n\n**C5.**\n(a) CPI = 20,000/24,000 = **0.833**; SPI = 20,000/25,000 = **0.80**; EAC = 50,000/0.833 = **$60,000**; ETC = 60,000 - 24,000 = **$36,000**; VAC = 50,000 - 60,000 = **-$10,000** [3]. Action: the project is behind schedule and over budget; report to the sponsor, find the causes, reduce scope (MoSCoW), and re-baseline [1].\n(b) NPV = -15,000 + 6,000 × (1/1.1 + 1/1.21 + 1/1.331) = -15,000 + 6,000 × 2.4869 = **-$79** (about zero but negative). IRR ≈ **9.7 percent**, slightly below 10 percent. Break-even benefit = 15,000/2.4869 = **$6,032 per year**. Decision: the project is marginal; it should not be accepted on financial grounds alone unless the benefit estimate is likely to be higher (with $6,500 per year the NPV would be +$1,165) or there are strategic benefits; test the sensitivity of the benefit estimate [4].\n(c) n = z² p(1 - p)/E² = 1.96² × 0.21/0.05² = **323** respondents [2].\n","notes":"# Final Year Project I: Complete Lecture Notes\n\nPrerequisites: all earlier years, in particular engineering communication and ethics, probability and statistics, and at least one design-oriented course. Final Year Project I (FYP I) takes you from a topic idea to an approved proposal, a literature review, a justified design and a realistic plan. The second semester (FYP II) implements, tests and defends it. Every number in the worked examples (critical path, PERT, earned value, NPV, sample sizes, uncertainty, statistics) was computed in Python so you can reproduce it with your own project data.\n\n## Chapter 1. What a Final Year Project Is\n\n### 1.1 Purpose and assessment\nThe project shows that you can **define a problem, apply engineering knowledge, make and justify design decisions under constraints, plan and execute the work, evaluate the result honestly and communicate it**. It is assessed on technical depth, independence, planning and process, analysis of results, and the quality of the written report and presentation. Typical FYP I deliverables: a proposal, a literature review, requirements and a conceptual design, a project plan, preliminary results (a simulation or feasibility test), a logbook and an oral presentation.\n\n### 1.2 Types of project\n- **Design and build** (a device, circuit, embedded or mechatronic system).\n- **Software or data systems** (an application, a machine-learning model, a tool).\n- **Research and simulation** (modelling, comparing methods, algorithm development).\n- **Analysis and case study** (evaluation of a system, a standard or a technology).\n- **Entrepreneurial or product development** (market need, prototype, business case).\nMost good projects mix types: a design that is also evaluated against a baseline with measured data.\n\n### 1.3 Choosing a topic and a supervisor\nChoose a problem that is (a) **interesting enough to sustain 9 months**, (b) **feasible** in time, budget, equipment and your skills, (c) **bounded** (a clear scope), and (d) **assessable** (you can measure success). Test an idea with five questions: Who needs this? What exists already? What exactly will I deliver? How will I know it works? What could stop me? Discuss with your supervisor early, agree meeting frequency (weekly or fortnightly), and write down what you agreed after every meeting.\n\n## Chapter 2. Problem Definition and the Proposal\n\n### 2.1 The problem statement\nState the **problem**, the **context**, the **gap** and the **impact** in a few sentences. Example: \"Greenhouse growers in region X lose 8 to 15 percent of crops to undetected humidity swings. Existing controllers cost more than $2,000 and need mains power. This project will design a low-cost, solar-powered sensing and control node costing under $60 that keeps relative humidity within ±5 percent.\" Notice that it includes a measurable target.\n\n### 2.2 Aims, objectives and success criteria\nThe **aim** is the overall purpose; **objectives** are specific, ordered steps that achieve it. Write objectives that are **SMART**: Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant, Time-bound. Weak: \"Improve the controller.\" Strong: \"By week 10 design a controller that holds humidity within ±5 percent in a simulated greenhouse model with a settling time under 5 minutes.\" Each objective should map to a deliverable and to a test.\n\n### 2.3 Scope, assumptions and constraints\nState what is **in scope** and **out of scope**, the assumptions you rely on, and the constraints: budget, time, equipment, standards, safety, ethics, environmental and social aspects. A clear scope protects you from scope creep, the commonest reason projects slip.\n\n### 2.4 Structure of a proposal\nTitle; abstract (150 to 250 words); introduction and background; problem statement; aim and objectives; literature review summary; proposed methodology and design approach; work plan (Gantt chart and milestones); resources and budget; risk assessment; expected outcomes and evaluation criteria; ethics and safety statement; references. Typical length: 8 to 15 pages. A reader who knows nothing about your topic should be able to decide whether the project is worthwhile and achievable.\n\n## Chapter 3. The Literature Review\n\n### 3.1 Purpose\nA literature review (a) shows what is already known, (b) identifies methods and results you can build on, (c) reveals a **gap** that justifies the project, and (d) prevents duplication. It is a **critical synthesis**, not a list of summaries: organize by theme, compare approaches, and judge their strengths and weaknesses.\n\n### 3.2 Search strategy\nStart with review articles and textbooks, then use IEEE Xplore, ACM Digital Library, ScienceDirect, Scopus, Web of Science and Google Scholar. Build queries with Boolean operators and synonyms, for example `(\"humidity control\" OR \"climate control\") AND greenhouse AND (\"low-cost\" OR \"solar\") AND (IoT OR microcontroller)`. Use **snowballing**: read the references of good papers (backward) and look at who cites them (forward). Record every search (date, database, query, number of results).\n\n**Screening funnel.** A typical review screens 1,200 results by title, keeps about 300, reads 80 abstracts, and ends with about 35 papers read in full: a yield of 35/1200 = 2.9 percent. Define inclusion and exclusion criteria in advance (date range, peer-reviewed, relevance) so your selection can be defended.\n\n### 3.3 Reading and note-taking\nRead in passes: title and abstract, then introduction and conclusion, then methods and results, then details. For each paper record: problem, method, data or setup, key results (with numbers), limitations, and relevance to your project. A **synthesis matrix** (rows = papers, columns = approach, performance, cost, limitation) makes comparison and gap-finding quick. Evaluate quality: venue and peer review, sample size, reproducibility, conflicts of interest, and whether the claims follow from the evidence.\n\n### 3.4 Writing the review\nStructure: introduction to the area; thematic sections (for example sensing methods, control strategies, power supplies); comparison of approaches; summary of gaps; and how your project addresses them. Use your own words, cite every idea you did not originate, and give your own critical assessment (\"Method A reports ±3 percent accuracy but was tested only at one temperature\").\n\n### 3.5 Citation and academic integrity\nUse a consistent style (IEEE numeric in engineering). Example: [1] A. Author, B. Author, \"Title of paper,\" *Journal Abbrev.*, vol. 12, no. 3, pp. 100-110, 2024. Cite sources inside the text as [1]; manage them with Zotero, Mendeley or BibTeX. **Plagiarism** (presenting others' words, figures or ideas as your own, including AI-generated text you do not disclose) is misconduct. Quote sparingly and mark quotations; paraphrase and cite; follow the institution's policy on generative AI, and never cite a source you have not checked. Reusing your own earlier work without disclosure is also self-plagiarism.\n\n## Chapter 4. Requirements and Specification\n\n### 4.1 Stakeholders and needs\nList the people affected: users, clients, supervisors, maintainers, regulators. Elicit needs through interviews, surveys, observation and study of existing products. Turn vague needs into **engineering requirements**.\n\n### 4.2 Good requirements\nA requirement is **necessary, unambiguous, measurable, verifiable, feasible and traceable**. \"The device shall be fast\" is bad; \"The device shall report humidity within 2 s of a 10 percent step change\" is good. Categories: **functional** (what the system does), **performance** (speed, accuracy, range), **interface** (protocols, connectors), **environmental** (temperature, ingress protection), **safety**, **cost**, **power**, **regulatory**, **maintainability**. Prioritize with **MoSCoW** (Must, Should, Could, Won't this time).\n\n### 4.3 Traceability and verification\nNumber each requirement (R1, R2, ...) and link it to a design element and a test (a **traceability matrix**). **Verification** asks \"did we build it right?\" (does it meet the specification); **validation** asks \"did we build the right thing?\" (does it meet the user's real need). Plan the acceptance test for each requirement while writing it.\n\n### 4.4 Standards, constraints and sustainability\nIdentify applicable standards (IEC/ISO/IEEE, electrical safety, EMC, radio regulations, coding standards). Consider health and safety, environmental impact, lifecycle (materials, energy, recycling), social and cultural aspects, and economics. Engineering accreditation expects explicit treatment of these constraints.\n\n## Chapter 5. Design Method\n\n### 5.1 From concepts to a chosen design\n1. Define functions (what must the system do?) and decompose into subsystems.\n2. Generate several concepts for each subsystem (brainstorm, survey the literature, morphological chart).\n3. Evaluate concepts against the requirements with a **decision matrix**.\n4. Choose, refine, model and simulate.\n5. Review the design with your supervisor before building.\n\n### 5.2 Weighted decision matrix (worked example)\nCriteria and weights (sum to 1): cost 0.3, performance 0.4, risk 0.2, time 0.1. Scores out of 10: option A = (7, 6, 8, 9), option B = (9, 8, 5, 6), option C = (6, 9, 6, 5). Weighted totals: A = 0.3·7 + 0.4·6 + 0.2·8 + 0.1·9 = **7.0**; B = 2.7 + 3.2 + 1.0 + 0.6 = **7.5**; C = 1.8 + 3.6 + 1.2 + 0.5 = **7.1**. Option B wins. **Sensitivity check:** with weights (0.4, 0.3, 0.2, 0.1) the totals are A 7.1, B 7.6, C 6.8 (B still wins); with equal weights (0.25 each) they are A 7.5, B 7.0, C 6.5, so **A** wins. A decision that flips with the weights is fragile: explain why your weights are justified (for example, performance matters most to the client) and report the sensitivity.\n\n### 5.3 Architecture and modelling\nDraw a **block diagram** with inputs, outputs and interfaces; a **data-flow diagram** or UML (use case, class, sequence) for software; a circuit schematic or mechanical sketch for hardware. Define interfaces precisely (voltage levels, protocols, message formats) so subsystems can be built and tested independently. Use **simulation** (SPICE, MATLAB/Simulink, Python, Gazebo, a network simulator) to explore options cheaply, and validate the model against at least one measurement or a published result.\n\n### 5.4 Prototyping and design for X\nPrototype the riskiest part first (the **spike**). Design for testability (test points, debug ports, logging), manufacturability, reliability, safety, and cost. For reliability: series components multiply failure rates. Three components with MTBF 50,000 h, 20,000 h and 100,000 h in series have a combined failure rate 1/50000 + 1/20000 + 1/100000 = 8×10⁻⁵ per hour, so **MTBF = 12,500 h** and the reliability for 1,000 h is e^(-0.08) = **0.923**. Two identical units each with MTBF 10,000 h in parallel (redundancy) raise 1,000 h reliability from 0.905 to **0.991**.\n\n### 5.5 Documentation and version control\nKeep a **logbook** (dated entries: what you did, results, decisions, problems), use Git for code, CAD and documents (commit early, with messages), back up to a second location, and number versions of design documents. Good records make the report easy to write and protect you if something is lost.\n\n## Chapter 6. Project Planning and Management\n\n### 6.1 Work breakdown structure and estimates\nDecompose the project into work packages (WBS) of 1 to 3 weeks each, with deliverables, owners and dependencies. Estimate effort bottom-up; add contingency (15 to 30 percent). Students typically underestimate integration and testing: plan at least 25 to 30 percent of the time for them. A team of 3 working 10 hours a week for 14 weeks supplies 3 × 14 × 10 = **420 person-hours**: check your WBS fits.\n\n### 6.2 Critical path method (worked example)\nTasks (weeks) and predecessors: A literature review 3 (none); B requirements 2 (A); C architecture 3 (B); D hardware design 4 (C); E software design 3 (C); F procure parts 2 (D); G prototype build 4 (F, E); H test plan 2 (C); I integration and test 3 (G, H); J report 3 (I).\n- **Forward pass** (earliest start/finish): A 0-3, B 3-5, C 5-8, D 8-12, E 8-11, H 8-10, F 12-14, G 14-18, I 18-21, J 21-24.\n- **Backward pass** (latest times, project length 24): J 21-24, I 18-21, G 14-18, F 12-14, D 8-12, E 11-14, H 16-18, C 5-8, B 3-5, A 0-3.\n- **Float** = latest start - earliest start: E has 3 weeks, H has 8 weeks, all others 0.\n- **Critical path**: A-B-C-D-F-G-I-J = 3 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 2 + 4 + 3 + 3 = **24 weeks**. Any delay on these tasks delays the project; E and H have float and can slip within their limits. To shorten the project, shorten critical tasks only (crashing D by one week saves one week until E's path becomes critical).\n\n### 6.3 PERT: planning under uncertainty\nFor each task estimate optimistic o, most likely m and pessimistic p durations. Expected duration = (o + 4m + p)/6; standard deviation = (p - o)/6. Example: o = 2, m = 4, p = 12 gives expected 5.0 and σ = 1.67 (note the expected value exceeds the most likely value because of the long tail). For a chain of five tasks with (o, m, p) = (2, 3, 6), (1, 2, 4), (2, 3, 7), (3, 5, 9), (2, 3, 8): expected durations 3.33, 2.17, 3.50, 5.33, 3.67 total **18.0 weeks**; the variances add: σ² = 0.44 + 0.25 + 0.69 + 1.00 + 1.00 = 3.39, so **σ = 1.84 weeks**. Assuming a normal distribution: P(finish within 16 weeks) = 0.14; within 18 weeks = 0.50; within 20 weeks = **0.86**; a 90 percent confidence date is 18 + 1.28 × 1.84 = **20.4 weeks**. Moral: a plan with no buffer has about a 50 percent chance of finishing on time; commit to the 80 to 90 percent date.\n\n### 6.4 Tracking progress: earned value\nPlanned value PV, earned value EV (budgeted cost of work done), actual cost AC. Example: BAC (budget at completion) = $20,000; 40 percent of the work should be done (PV = $8,000); 30 percent is done (EV = $6,000); you have spent $7,500 (AC). Then **schedule performance index SPI = EV/PV = 0.75** (behind schedule) and **cost performance index CPI = EV/AC = 0.80** (over budget). Cost variance CV = EV - AC = -$1,500; schedule variance SV = EV - PV = -$2,000. Forecast: **EAC = BAC/CPI = $25,000**, estimate to complete ETC = EAC - AC = $17,500, variance at completion = -$5,000. To finish on budget you would need TCPI = (BAC - EV)/(BAC - AC) = 1.12 (12 percent more efficient than so far), which is unrealistic: tell your supervisor early.\n\n### 6.5 Methodologies\n**Waterfall** (sequential phases) suits well-defined hardware; **iterative/agile** (short sprints, working increments, regular review) suits software and uncertain requirements; **V-model** pairs each design level with a test level; **stage-gate** reviews at milestones. Hybrid plans are common. Hold short regular meetings, keep a task board (Kanban), and record decisions.\n\n### 6.6 Risk management\nIdentify risks, score each by likelihood (1 to 5) times impact (1 to 5), plan mitigation and monitor. Example ranking: scope creep (4 × 4 = 16), supplier delay (4 × 3 = 12), key member unavailable (2 × 5 = 10), sensor noise (3 × 3 = 9), data loss (2 × 4 = 8). Mitigations: freeze scope after the proposal, order long-lead parts in week 2, document everything so work can be handed over, test the sensor early with a spike, back up daily. Expected monetary value of a risk = probability × cost: 0.3 × $4,000 + 0.1 × $10,000 = **$2,200** of contingency. Keep a risk register and update it monthly.\n\n## Chapter 7. Budget and Feasibility\n\n### 7.1 Bill of materials and contingency\nExample BOM: 5 microcontroller boards at $12 = $60; 8 sensors at $6.50 = $52; 1 PCB fabrication $45; 2 enclosures at $15 = $30; total **$187**. Add 15 percent contingency: **$215.05**. Include shipping, tax, tools, software licences, testing equipment time and spare parts. Check the budget limit with the department.\n\n### 7.2 Economic evaluation (product-oriented projects)\nFor a project costing $12,000 that returns $5,000 per year for 4 years, with a 10 percent discount rate: **NPV** = -12,000 + 5,000(1/1.1 + 1/1.1² + 1/1.1³ + 1/1.1⁴) = **+$3,849** (positive, so worthwhile); **payback** = 2.4 years (simple); the discounted payback falls in year 3 (cumulative discounted cash flow turns positive between -3,322 in year 2 and +434 in year 3); **IRR = 24.1 percent**; **ROI** over four years = (20,000 - 12,000)/12,000 = 67 percent. Compare against alternatives and test sensitivity to the benefit estimate.\n\n### 7.3 Technical feasibility\nShow with calculation, simulation or a quick experiment that the central idea can work: a power budget, a link budget, a timing analysis, a throughput estimate or a proof-of-concept measurement. Present the evidence in the proposal and the first progress report.\n\n## Chapter 8. Research Method, Experiments and Data\n\n### 8.1 Hypotheses and experiment design\nState what you expect and how you will test it: \"Controller B reduces settling time by at least 20 percent relative to controller A.\" Choose the **independent variable** (what you change), **dependent variable** (what you measure) and **controls** (what stays fixed). Randomize order, repeat measurements, and include a baseline. Record the setup so another person can reproduce the work.\n\n### 8.2 Measurement uncertainty\nEvery measurement has uncertainty. For a product of independent quantities the relative uncertainties add in quadrature: V = I × R with I = 2.00 ± 0.02 A (1 percent) and R = 10.0 ± 0.1 Ω (1 percent) gives V = 20.0 V with relative uncertainty √(1² + 1²) = 1.41 percent, i.e. **±0.28 V** (the worst-case linear sum would be ±0.40 V). For P = V²/R with V = 12.0 ± 0.1 V and R = 48.0 ± 0.5 Ω: P = 3.00 W and the relative uncertainty is √((2 × 0.83)² + 1.04²) = 1.97 percent, so **±0.059 W**. Report results as value ± uncertainty with the confidence level and units, and distinguish **accuracy** (closeness to the true value) from **precision** (repeatability).\n\n### 8.3 Repeated measurements and confidence intervals\nTen measurements of a voltage: 9.8, 10.1, 10.0, 9.9, 10.2, 10.1, 9.9, 10.0, 10.3, 9.7 V give mean 10.00 V and sample standard deviation s = 0.183 V. The standard error is s/√n = 0.0577 V. With t(0.975, 9) = 2.262 the 95 percent confidence interval is 10.00 ± 0.131, i.e. **(9.87, 10.13) V**. Quote the interval rather than the number of decimals the calculator shows.\n\n### 8.4 Comparing two designs\nSettling times (s) over six runs each: design A = 12.1, 11.8, 12.4, 12.0, 12.3, 11.9 (mean 12.08, s = 0.23); design B = 12.9, 13.1, 12.7, 13.3, 12.8, 13.0 (mean 12.97, s = 0.22). Difference = 0.88 s with standard error 0.129 s. Welch's t-test gives t = -6.83, about 10 degrees of freedom, p = 4.7 × 10⁻⁵, so the difference is statistically significant at the 5 percent level. Also report the **effect size** and whether it matters in practice, and be careful with multiple comparisons and with data you tuned on.\n\n### 8.5 Sample size\nTo estimate a mean within ±E at 95 percent confidence: n = (1.96 σ/E)². With σ = 5 and E = 1: n = 96.04, so **97** samples. For a proportion with the worst case p = 0.5: n = 1.96² × 0.25/E²; E = 0.05 gives **385**, E = 0.03 gives **1,068**. Plan sample sizes before collecting data.\n\n### 8.6 Reliability of your conclusions\nCheck **inter-rater agreement** with Cohen's kappa when humans label data: observed agreement 0.90 with chance agreement 0.6 × 0.7 + 0.4 × 0.3 = 0.54 gives κ = (0.90 - 0.54)/(1 - 0.54) = 0.78. For machine-learning projects keep training, validation and test data separate and report variance across seeds. Archive raw data and scripts; plot data before summarizing; document anomalies instead of deleting them.\n\n## Chapter 9. Ethics, Safety and Professional Practice\n\n- **Ethics**: honesty in data and reporting, no fabrication or selective reporting, fair credit to collaborators, respect for confidentiality. Projects involving people (surveys, user tests) need ethical approval, informed consent, anonymization and secure storage. Follow data-protection law.\n- **Safety**: risk assessment for lab work (electrical hazards, batteries, lasers, moving parts), supervision rules, protective equipment, and safe design (fuses, enclosures, emergency stops). Document the hazards you identify and the controls.\n- **Intellectual property**: know who owns the work (university, sponsor, you); respect licences of code, libraries and datasets (open-source obligations); do not use proprietary material without permission.\n- **Sustainability and society**: energy use, materials, e-waste, accessibility, and the impact of failure on users. Consider the United Nations Sustainable Development Goals where relevant.\n- **Responsible use of AI tools**: disclose assistance as your institution requires, verify every claim and citation, and never submit text you cannot explain. You remain responsible for correctness.\n\n## Chapter 10. Communication: Proposal, Report and Presentation\n\n### 10.1 Report structure\nTitle page; abstract; table of contents and lists of figures/tables; introduction; literature review; requirements and design; method; results; discussion (interpretation, limitations, comparison with the literature); conclusion and future work; references; appendices (code, schematics, data). Write for a technically educated reader who has not seen your work. A 12,000-word report is about 40 pages at 300 words per page plus figures.\n\n### 10.2 Writing well\nOne idea per paragraph; topic sentence first; active voice where possible; define symbols and abbreviations at first use; number and caption every figure and table and refer to them in the text; label axes with units; use consistent notation and SI units; report numbers with sensible precision. Draft early and revise: write the methods and literature review as you go. Ask a classmate to read it for clarity.\n\n### 10.3 The oral presentation\nTypical 10 to 15 minutes. Structure: problem and motivation (2 slides), objectives, literature gap, approach and design, plan and risks, preliminary results, conclusion. Aim for one idea per slide, large figures, minimal text, a rehearsed timing, and a prepared answer to \"what is your contribution and how will you know it works?\". Practise questions on assumptions, alternatives, risks and ethics. A poster should be readable from 1.5 m with a clear flow from problem to result.\n\n## Chapter 11. Working with Supervisors and Teams\n- Prepare a short agenda or progress note before each meeting (done, problems, plan, decisions needed) and send minutes afterwards.\n- Raise problems early: supervisors can help with a delayed part, but not with a problem revealed in the last week.\n- In a team, assign roles, agree standards (code style, document templates), use shared tools, and review each other's work. Resolve conflict by discussing facts and agreed responsibilities; involve the supervisor if needed. Record each member's contribution.\n- Manage time with weekly goals, protected work blocks, and a buffer. Avoid the **90 percent syndrome** (an almost-finished item that is never closed) by defining done precisely.\n\n## Chapter 12. FYP I Checklist and Common Mistakes\n\n**Checklist at the end of FYP I:** approved proposal with SMART objectives; literature review with a stated gap and 25 or more relevant, well-chosen references; requirements with measurable targets and a traceability table; concept selection with a documented decision matrix and sensitivity; architecture and interface definitions; a feasibility result (simulation or proof-of-concept) with numbers; Gantt chart with critical path and milestones; risk register; budget with contingency; ethics and safety statement; logbook and version control in place.\n\n**Common mistakes.** A topic that is too broad or a goal that cannot be measured; reading papers without synthesizing them; copying text or figures without citation; requirements that are vague (\"user friendly\"); choosing a design before comparing alternatives; no buffer in the plan and underestimating testing and integration; starting the build before the riskiest part is proved; no baseline to compare against; reporting results without uncertainty or statistics; keeping no logbook or backups; leaving the report to the last week; and hiding problems from the supervisor.\n","plan":"# Final Year Project I: 14-Week Study Plan and Resources\n\nTime budget: 10 to 14 hours per week on your project (about 2 hours of meetings, reading and writing, and the rest on design, simulation and preliminary experiments), plus 1 hour reviewing these notes with the app's quizzes. The plan below is a model timeline for one semester; adjust it to your institution's milestone dates and your supervisor's advice.\n\n## Books and guides\n- Gerard Voland, *Engineering by Design*; Karl Ulrich and Steven Eppinger, *Product Design and Development*; George Dieter and Linda Schmidt, *Engineering Design* (concept generation, selection, requirements).\n- Colin Neville, *The Complete Guide to Referencing and Avoiding Plagiarism*; Kate Turabian and the IEEE Editorial Style Manual for citation.\n- Nigel Gilbert and Paul Stoneman on research methods; Justin Zobel, *Writing for Computer Science*; Strunk and White or Steven Pinker, *The Sense of Style*, for clear writing.\n- Project Management Institute, *PMBOK Guide* (planning, risk, earned value); Eric Verzuh, *The Fast Forward MBA in Project Management*.\n- Douglas Montgomery, *Design and Analysis of Experiments*, and John Taylor, *An Introduction to Error Analysis*, for measurement and statistics.\n\n## Free resources\n- University writing centres and library guides on literature searching, referencing and avoiding plagiarism.\n- IEEE Xplore, Google Scholar, Scopus and ResearchGate for papers; Zotero, Mendeley or BibTeX for reference management; Overleaf (LaTeX) or a word processor with a thesis template.\n- Git and GitHub/GitLab for version control; Notion, Trello or GitHub Projects for task boards; ProjectLibre or a spreadsheet for Gantt charts and critical-path calculation.\n- NIST and ISO/IEC/IEEE standard summaries; the United Nations Sustainable Development Goals pages for impact framing.\n- Young Engineers' competitions, past FYP archives in your library (read two good reports to see the expected level).\n\n## Week by week\n\n**Week 1: Topic, supervisor and problem.** Brainstorm topics, check feasibility, meet potential supervisors, and agree a meeting schedule. Deliverable: a one-page project idea (problem, users, gap, feasibility, what you will deliver).\n\n**Week 2: Problem statement, aim and objectives.** Write the statement with a measurable target; draft SMART objectives and a scope with out-of-scope items and assumptions. Deliverable: the problem statement, aim and objectives agreed with the supervisor.\n\n**Week 3: Literature search.** Design Boolean search strings, search at least three databases, log the searches, and begin screening. Deliverable: a search log and a screening table (records, stage counts, yield).\n\n**Week 4: Reading and synthesis.** Read 20 to 40 papers in passes; fill a synthesis matrix; draft the review's structure and the research gap. Deliverable: the synthesis matrix and a review outline.\n\n**Week 5: Requirements.** Identify stakeholders, write measurable requirements, apply MoSCoW, check standards and constraints, create the traceability table. Deliverable: the requirements specification v1.\n\n**Week 6: Concepts and selection.** Generate alternatives for each subsystem, define criteria and weights, build a decision matrix and test its sensitivity. Deliverable: a concept-selection document with the rationale.\n\n**Week 7: Proposal draft and midterm review.** Assemble the proposal (background, objectives, review summary, method, plan, budget, risks, ethics). Deliverable: the full proposal draft for supervisor feedback.\n\n**Week 8: Architecture and interfaces.** Block diagrams, data flows, interface definitions, power and timing budgets, UML if software. Deliverable: the architecture document.\n\n**Week 9: Modelling, simulation and feasibility.** Build a simulation or a proof-of-concept spike of the riskiest part; check the results against theory or a published reference. Deliverable: preliminary results with numbers and uncertainty.\n\n**Week 10: Planning.** Work breakdown structure, effort estimates with contingency, Gantt chart, critical path, PERT for uncertain tasks, milestones, and a plan for earned-value tracking. Deliverable: the project plan with a critical path.\n\n**Week 11: Risk, budget and procurement.** Risk register with scores and mitigations, bill of materials with 15 to 20 percent contingency, long-lead orders placed. Deliverable: the risk register and budget.\n\n**Week 12: Ethics, safety and test plan.** Ethical approval if needed, hazard assessment, data management plan, verification and validation test plan linked to the requirements. Deliverable: the ethics/safety statement and the test plan.\n\n**Week 13: Final report and presentation preparation.** Complete the report (introduction, review, requirements, design, plan, preliminary results, conclusions), proofread, check references, rehearse the presentation. Deliverable: the FYP I report and slides.\n\n**Week 14: Presentation and hand-over to FYP II.** Present, answer questions, record feedback, and revise the plan for FYP II (a task list for the first four weeks). Deliverable: the presentation, the final report, and the revised FYP II plan.\n\n## Assessment pattern used in this course\nThree assignments (proposal and literature review; requirements, concept selection and reliability; planning, cost, statistics and professional practice), a class test, and a three-hour final. Reproduce every calculation in the notes (critical path, PERT, earned value, NPV, sample size, uncertainty) with your own project data.\n\n## Study tips\n- Write something every week; the report is the project's memory.\n- Prove the riskiest idea early with a small experiment before committing the whole plan to it.\n- Keep every decision, assumption and number in the logbook, with the date and the reason.\n- Report numbers with uncertainty and a baseline; avoid \"better\" without a measured comparison.\n- Meet your supervisor with a short written update every time, and raise problems the week you see them.\n","test":"# Final Year Project I: Class Test\n\nDuration: 60 minutes. Calculator allowed, no notes. Total: 60 marks. Section A: 20 multiple-choice questions (1 mark each). Section B: 4 problems (10 marks each). Useful: t₀.₉₇₅ with 4 degrees of freedom = 2.776; z₀.₉₇₅ = 1.96.\n\n## Section A: Multiple choice (20 marks)\n\n**1.** In SMART objectives, the letters stand for\nA) Simple, Modern, Affordable, Reliable, Tested  B) Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant, Time-bound  C) Safe, Mature, Accurate, Robust, Tidy  D) Scope, Method, Analysis, Results, Timeline\n\n**2.** The main purpose of a literature review is to\nA) copy the best method  B) increase the page count  C) synthesize existing work and identify a gap that justifies the project  D) list every paper ever published\n\n**3.** In a database search, joining two concepts with AND\nA) narrows the search to records containing both  B) sorts by date  C) widens the search  D) excludes both\n\n**4.** A good requirement is\nA) a wish list item  B) written after testing  C) measurable and verifiable  D) as general as possible\n\n**5.** In MoSCoW prioritization, \"Must\" means\nA) a feature that may be dropped  B) a feature excluded for now  C) a requirement without which the project fails  D) a nice-to-have feature\n\n**6.** Verification asks\nA) \"Did we build the right thing for the user?\"  B) \"Did we build it right, meeting the specification?\"  C) \"Is it profitable?\"  D) \"Is it legal?\"\n\n**7.** The critical path is\nA) any path through the first task  B) the shortest path  C) the path with the most tasks  D) the longest path with zero float that sets the project duration\n\n**8.** The float (slack) of a task equals\nA) its cost  B) latest start minus earliest start  C) duration squared  D) earliest finish plus latest start\n\n**9.** In PERT the expected duration is\nA) (o + 4m + p)/6  B) (p - o)/6  C) the pessimistic value  D) (o + m + p)/3\n\n**10.** Compared with its weakest component, a series system of many components has\nA) infinite MTBF  B) the same reliability  C) higher reliability  D) lower reliability\n\n**11.** The cost performance index is\nA) EV/AC  B) AC/EV  C) EV/PV  D) PV/EV\n\n**12.** An SPI below 1 means the project is\nA) under budget  B) behind schedule  C) on schedule  D) ahead of schedule\n\n**13.** A positive NPV generally means\nA) the IRR is negative  B) the payback is infinite  C) the project adds value at the chosen discount rate  D) the project should be rejected\n\n**14.** Which is plagiarism?\nA) quoting with quotation marks and a reference  B) using someone else's text or figure without credit  C) citing a paper correctly  D) paraphrasing with a citation\n\n**15.** IEEE referencing in engineering reports uses\nA) footnotes only  B) numbered citations such as [1]  C) no citations  D) author-date only\n\n**16.** A Gantt chart mainly shows\nA) statistical significance  B) tasks against a time scale with dependencies and milestones  C) cost-benefit  D) circuit connections\n\n**17.** A simple risk score is\nA) the number of team members  B) impact minus likelihood  C) likelihood times impact  D) likelihood plus cost\n\n**18.** Increasing the sample size n generally makes a 95 percent confidence interval for a mean\nA) wider  B) narrower  C) unchanged  D) biased\n\n**19.** Welch's t-test is used to\nA) fit a line  B) check spelling  C) compare two means without assuming equal variances  D) compute a median\n\n**20.** The main value of a project logbook is to\nA) fill space in the appendix  B) record dated work, decisions and results for reproducibility and evidence  C) replace the report  D) impress the examiner\n\n## Section B: Problems (40 marks)\n\n**B1. Objectives and screening (10 marks)** (a) Rewrite \"Improve the plant monitor\" as a SMART objective for a greenhouse humidity node [4]. (b) A literature search returns 1,500 records; 300 pass the title screen, 75 the abstract screen and 30 are read in full. Give the yield at each stage and overall, and the reading time at 2 hours per paper [4]. (c) Give one inclusion criterion [2].\n\n**B2. Critical path (10 marks)** Tasks (weeks): A 2 (none); B 3 (A); C 4 (A); D 2 (B, C); E 3 (D). (a) Compute the earliest start and finish of every task [4]. (b) Give the critical path and the project duration [3]. (c) Compute the float of B [3].\n\n**B3. Concept selection and earned value (10 marks)** (a) Two designs are scored on cost (weight 0.4) and performance (weight 0.6): X = (8, 6) and Y = (5, 9). Compute the weighted scores and choose [3]. With equal weights which design wins? [2] (b) A $10,000 project has PV = $6,000, EV = $5,000, AC = $5,500. Compute CPI, SPI and the forecast EAC = BAC/CPI, and interpret [5].\n\n**B4. Measurement (10 marks)** (a) Power P = V × I with V = 12.0 V ± 1 percent and I = 0.50 A ± 2 percent. Compute P and its uncertainty (quadrature) [3]. (b) Five readings of a length: 20.1, 19.8, 20.3, 20.0, 19.9 mm. Find the mean, sample standard deviation, standard error and 95 percent confidence interval [5]. (c) Say why an uncertainty statement is better than quoting all calculator digits [2].\n\n## Answer key and solutions\n\n### Section A\n1. B 2. C 3. A 4. C 5. C 6. B 7. D 8. B 9. A 10. D 11. A 12. B 13. C 14. B 15. B 16. B 17. C 18. B 19. C 20. B\n\n### Section B\n\n**B1.**\n(a) Example: \"By week 10, design and simulate a controller for the greenhouse node that holds relative humidity within ±5 percent of the setpoint with a settling time below 5 minutes, and report the results in a simulation report.\" Specific (controller, humidity), measurable (±5 percent, 5 minutes), achievable (simulation), relevant (project aim), time-bound (week 10) [4].\n(b) Title screen 300/1500 = **20 percent**; abstract screen 75/300 = **25 percent**; full-text 30/75 = **40 percent**; overall 30/1500 = **2 percent** [3]. Reading time = 30 × 2 = **60 hours** [1].\n(c) Any one: peer-reviewed; published in the last 10 years; reports measured results; directly addresses humidity control in greenhouses [2].\n\n**B2.**\n(a) A: ES 0, EF 2. B: ES 2, EF 5. C: ES 2, EF 6. D: ES 6 (must wait for C), EF 8. E: ES 8, EF 11 [4].\n(b) Paths: A-B-D-E = 2 + 3 + 2 + 3 = 10; A-C-D-E = 2 + 4 + 2 + 3 = 11. Critical path **A-C-D-E, duration 11 weeks** [3].\n(c) B's latest finish is 6 (D must start at 6) so its latest start is 3; float = LS - ES = 3 - 2 = **1 week** [3].\n\n**B3.**\n(a) X = 0.4 × 8 + 0.6 × 6 = **6.8**; Y = 0.4 × 5 + 0.6 × 9 = 2.0 + 5.4 = **7.4**; choose **Y** [3]. Equal weights: X = (8 + 6)/2 = 7.0; Y = (5 + 9)/2 = 7.0; a **tie**, so the weights decide (the choice is sensitive) [2].\n(b) CPI = 5,000/5,500 = **0.909**; SPI = 5,000/6,000 = **0.833**; EAC = 10,000/0.909 = **$11,000** [3]. The project is behind schedule (about 17 percent) and over cost; if the trend continues it will cost about $1,000 more than planned, so corrective action is needed [2].\n\n**B4.**\n(a) P = 12.0 × 0.50 = **6.0 W**; relative uncertainty √(1² + 2²) = 2.24 percent → **±0.13 W** [3].\n(b) Mean = (20.1 + 19.8 + 20.3 + 20.0 + 19.9)/5 = **20.02 mm**; deviations 0.08, -0.22, 0.28, -0.02, -0.12, squares sum 0.148, s = √(0.148/4) = **0.192 mm**; SE = 0.192/√5 = **0.086 mm**; CI = 20.02 ± 2.776 × 0.086 = 20.02 ± 0.24 = **(19.78, 20.26) mm** [5].\n(c) The uncertainty tells the reader how much of the digits is meaningful and allows fair comparison and decision making; extra digits imply precision that the measurement does not have [2].\n"},mcq:[{"q":"In SMART objectives, the letters stand for","options":["Simple, Modern, Affordable, Reliable, Tested","Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant, Time-bound","Safe, Mature, Accurate, Robust, Tidy","Scope, Method, Analysis, Results, Timeline"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.025,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The main purpose of a literature review is to","options":["copy the best method","increase the page count","synthesize existing work and identify a gap that justifies the project","list every paper ever published"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.075,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"In a database search, joining two concepts with AND","options":["narrows the search to records containing both","sorts by date","widens the search","excludes both"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.125,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A good requirement is","options":["a wish list item","written after testing","measurable and verifiable","as general as possible"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.175,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"In MoSCoW prioritization, \"Must\" means","options":["a feature that may be dropped","a feature excluded for now","a requirement without which the project fails","a nice-to-have feature"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.225,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Verification asks","options":["\"Did we build the right thing for the user?\"","\"Did we build it right, meeting the specification?\"","\"Is it profitable?\"","\"Is it legal?\""],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.275,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The critical path is","options":["any path through the first task","the shortest path","the path with the most tasks","the longest path with zero float that sets the project duration"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.325,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The float (slack) of a task equals","options":["its cost","latest start minus earliest start","duration squared","earliest finish plus latest start"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.375,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"In PERT the expected duration is","options":["(o + 4m + p)/6","(p - o)/6","the pessimistic value","(o + m + p)/3"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.425,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Compared with its weakest component, a series system of many components has","options":["infinite MTBF","the same reliability","higher reliability","lower reliability"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.475,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The cost performance index is","options":["EV/AC","AC/EV","EV/PV","PV/EV"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.525,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"An SPI below 1 means the project is","options":["under budget","behind schedule","on schedule","ahead of schedule"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.575,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A positive NPV generally means","options":["the IRR is negative","the payback is infinite","the project adds value at the chosen discount rate","the project should be rejected"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.625,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Which is plagiarism?","options":["quoting with quotation marks and a reference","using someone else's text or figure without credit","citing a paper correctly","paraphrasing with a citation"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.675,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"IEEE referencing in engineering reports uses","options":["footnotes only","numbered citations such as [1]","no citations","author-date only"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.725,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A Gantt chart mainly shows","options":["statistical significance","tasks against a time scale with dependencies and milestones","cost-benefit","circuit connections"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.775,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A simple risk score is","options":["the number of team members","impact minus likelihood","likelihood times impact","likelihood plus cost"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.825,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Increasing the sample size n generally makes a 95 percent confidence interval for a mean","options":["wider","narrower","unchanged","biased"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.875,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Welch's t-test is used to","options":["fit a line","check spelling","compare two means without assuming equal variances","compute a median"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.925,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The main value of a project logbook is to","options":["fill space in the appendix","record dated work, decisions and results for reproducibility and evidence","replace the report","impress the examiner"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.975,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The aim of a project differs from its objectives in that the aim is","options":["a single task","the budget","the reference list","the overall purpose, achieved through a set of specific objectives"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.025,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Which is the best objective?","options":["Study sensors","By week 12, build a prototype that measures temperature within ±0.5 °C over 0 to 50 °C","Improve the design","Make a good product"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.075,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Snowballing in a literature search means","options":["copying a review","following reference lists and citations from known good papers","deleting old papers","searching only on Google"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.125,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A synthesis matrix is used to","options":["compare sources on common criteria and reveal gaps","check spelling","draw circuits","schedule tasks"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.175,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A traceability matrix links","options":["papers to authors","team members to hours","requirements to design elements and tests","tasks to money only"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.225,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Validation checks that","options":["the code compiles","the document is formatted","the product meets the real needs of the user","the schedule is met"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.275,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Float on a non-critical task means","options":["it is optional","it is more expensive","it must start first","it can be delayed by that amount without delaying the project"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.325,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"PERT standard deviation of one task is approximated by","options":["(p - o)/6","m/6","(o + 4m + p)/6","(o + p)/2"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.375,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Adding a redundant parallel unit to a component generally","options":["removes all failures","raises reliability but adds cost, mass and complexity","lowers system reliability","has no effect"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.425,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"In earned value, CPI less than 1 indicates","options":["the work is costing more than planned","the project is ahead of schedule","the project is under budget","there is no risk"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.475,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A project with NPV less than zero at the required discount rate","options":["has an infinite payback","has IRR above the hurdle rate","should generally not be accepted on financial grounds alone","is risk free"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.525,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A risk register should contain","options":["the team's salaries","only completed risks","just the project title","risks with likelihood, impact, owner and mitigation"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.575,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The best way to avoid scope creep is to","options":["agree and freeze the scope and control changes formally","accept every request","never talk to the client","delete the plan"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.625,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Reporting a result with an uncertainty is important because it","options":["hides errors","replaces the method section","lengthens the report","shows how reliable the number is"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.675,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"For comparing the means of two independent samples with unequal variances use","options":["the median","a Welch t-test","a chi-square goodness-of-fit test","a linear regression"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.725,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Data leakage in a machine-learning project means","options":["the battery is empty","test information influences training or tuning","the data are too small","the server is hacked"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.775,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Which action is academically dishonest?","options":["discussing ideas with your supervisor","presenting another person's results as your own","citing sources","keeping a logbook"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.825,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Ethical approval is normally required when a project","options":["collects data from human participants","uses a microcontroller","has a Gantt chart","uses open-source libraries"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.875,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A good abstract","options":["lists every reference","summarizes problem, method, main results and conclusion in about 150 to 250 words","contains only background","is written before the project starts and never changed"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.925,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The best time to start writing the report is","options":["after the examiner's feedback","the final week","after the viva","from the start, drafting sections as work is done"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.975,"src":"Final exam"}]});
